// The stop-the-world safepoint — `root.zig`'s `safepointEntry` +
// `safepointImpl` (root.zig:638-750) IN FULL: the mutator handshake (#1629) and
// the COLLECTOR half (#1633), so a Bit-only `libbitrt.a` actually collects.
//
// It was missed by every earlier audit because it is not an `export fn`: root.zig
// publishes it with `comptime { @export(&safepointEntry, .{ .name =
// "bit_rt_safepoint" }); }` (root.zig:753), which a `grep 'export fn bit_rt_'`
// enumeration cannot see. It is also the least optional symbol in the ABI — both
// backends SYNTHESIZE a call to it at every loop back-edge (seed/codegen/x64.zig,
// seed/codegen/arm64.zig), so an image that does not define it does not link.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// WHY IT IS ITS OWN MODULE AND NOT `runtime/gc`'s (#1633 moved it here)
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// #1629 landed it in `runtime/gc` because the mutator handshake is entirely the
// World registry's, which is `../gc/gcworld.bit`'s. The COLLECTOR half cannot
// live there, and the reason is structural rather than stylistic: a collection
// must enumerate root sources owned by `runtime/chan` (buffered `is_ref` channel
// elements, ABI.md §11) and by `runtime/sched` (every other task's stack and
// every task's `arg`, §5), and `runtime/gc` may not import either — `chan`
// imports `../gc`, so the reverse edge is an import cycle (E0044). `extern
// function` is not an escape: it is not on the `@nosplit` allowlist (§10.3) and
// a Linux `--emit-obj` rejects it outright (E0078, the wall SEAM 1 hit at #1600).
//
// So the composition moves to a LEAF module that may import all three, and
// nothing imports it. `../gc/gcworld.bit` keeps sole ownership of the World
// block address and the current-mutator cache — duplicating that cache would let
// one thread hold two slots, which the rendezvous then waits for forever — and
// publishes the two reads this file needs (`worldBlockAddr`, `worldEnsureSelf`).
//
// `runtime/stw` holds ONE file, so the #1511 sort-order rule is trivially
// satisfied; every helper it calls is a pinned symbol in another object.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// THE PIN CYCLE, AND WHY NOTHING HERE CAN CALL ITSELF (#1367)
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// This is the pin-cycle trap (tests/rootpins.zig) in its purest form: the
// backends emit a call to `bit_rt_safepoint` at every loop back edge, so a
// definition of `bit_rt_safepoint` that contains a loop back edge calls ITSELF.
// Pre-rename that call lands harmlessly on the still-linked Zig, so nothing —
// not a build, not a link, not a test run — can see it until G2 (#1583) drops the
// `_root` infix, at which point it is an instant stack overflow in every program
// that loops.
//
// Two independent guarantees, both structural rather than asserted:
//
//   - `safepointEntry` is `@naked` and its body is a single `asm` block. §10.3.1
//     admits no locals, no control flow and no implicit prologue in a naked
//     function, and an `asm` payload is emitted verbatim, so there is no back
//     edge for codegen to attach a poll to. The emitted object carries no
//     relocation to any `bit_rt_` name (checked by `zig build rootpins`, which
//     performs the G2 rename symbolically on the emitted object).
//   - EVERY OTHER function here is `@nosplit`, which suppresses back-edge poll
//     emission outright (§10.3), and whose default-deny allowlist additionally
//     refuses any call to a non-`@nosplit` function (E0075) — so the guarantee is
//     transitive over everything they reach, including the objects this file
//     drives, without anyone having to re-derive the call graph. That matters
//     most for the loops below: this file contains five, and each of them would
//     otherwise be a recursive poll.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// THE ROOT SET, AND WHY IT IS ALL-OR-NOTHING
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// A collection that misses ONE root class does not crash — it sweeps a live
// object and corrupts the heap silently. So the five classes `root.zig`'s
// `scanRoots` (root.zig:406) funnels are enumerated here in the same order, and
// each names where it is implemented:
//
//   1. Buffered `is_ref` channel elements (§11) — `chanScanRegistryRoots`
//      (`../chan/chanreg.bit`, #1602). A buffered word is reachable only through
//      the ring while it sits unreceived. The ring is PROCESS memory, not a
//      GC-traced allocation, so its CONTENTS are the roots.
//   2. THIS thread's own frames, precisely (§4) — `gcScanSnapshot`
//      (`../gc/stackmap.bit`, #1633), walking the stack maps between
//      `stackMapsBegin()`/`stackMapsEnd()`.
//   3. Every OTHER stopped OS thread's frames, precisely — the same walk over
//      each parked mutator's published snapshot (`worldParkedFrame`). The world
//      is stopped, so each of those frames is valid and not moving.
//   4. Every OTHER task's stack, conservatively (§5) — claimed sound because a
//      parked task's callee-saved references are all spilled by the runtime
//      call chain that parked it. THAT CLAIM IS ONLY HALF TRUE HERE — see
//      class 7.
//   5. Every registered task's opaque `arg` (§5/§9) — a spawn thunk is a GC
//      object reachable ONLY through the pending task's `arg` until that task
//      runs and unpacks it.
//   6. Every registered task's per-task SCRATCH area (`stwScanScratch`, #1679).
//      THIS ONE HAS NO `root.zig` COUNTERPART. It exists because the port keeps
//      two live references (`scrElem`, the blocking channel value slot, and
//      `scrErr`, the pending-error object) in the `chanScratchWords` words at
//      the BASE of each task's stack, which is strictly BELOW `ctx.sp` and so
//      outside class 4's range for every task, always. Zig keeps the first as a
//      stack local (class 4 covers it) and the second in a `threadlocal`.
//   7. Every OTHER task's own saved register file, conservatively
//      (`stwScanCtx`, #1742). THIS ONE ALSO HAS NO `root.zig` COUNTERPART, and
//      it is the fix to class 4's soundness gap: `root.zig`'s `park` hands off
//      through an ordinary LLVM call chain, whose callee-saved spills for a
//      still-live value land on a callee's STACK FRAME and so are already
//      inside class 4's `[sp, stackTop)` walk. This port's `schedSwitch`
//      (`../sched/sched.bit`) is hand-written `asm`, and it saves the
//      callee-saved file into the TASK'S OWN CONTROL BLOCK instead — a
//      separate allocation from its stack, outside class 4's range by
//      construction. A GC reference the register allocator keeps live in one
//      of those registers across a park (measured: a `string`'s pointer held
//      in a callee-saved register across a `sleep()` retry loop, #1742) was
//      invisible to every class above and collected while still needed —
//      silently, the way this header warns every missed class does. `root.zig`
//      never needed this class because it never had this problem.
//
//   8. The RUNNING task's own stack, conservatively (`stwScanTasks`, #1832).
//      THIS ONE HAS NO `root.zig` COUNTERPART EITHER, and like classes 6 and 7
//      it exists because of a property of the PORT rather than an oversight.
//
//      Class 2 covers this stack precisely, and precisely is not enough here. A
//      stack map names REFERENCE-TYPED slots; an `int` slot that happens to hold
//      an object address is invisible to it by construction. The runtime is full
//      of exactly that: the ABI boundary FORCES a pinned symbol to take its
//      argument as a bare `int` (E0079 — a pinned symbol may not name a managed
//      type), so `bit_rt_chan_send` receives the element it is about to store as
//      an `int`, and holds it as one across its own loop.
//
//      And a collection CAN land there. Codegen synthesizes `bl
//      bit_rt_safepoint` at every loop back-edge, so any `bit_rt_*` implemented
//      in Bit and NOT marked `@nosplit` can be interrupted mid-operation
//      (#1656/#1658). Six such pins are on the channel path alone —
//      `bit_rt_chan_send/recv/make/close`, `bit_rt_select`,
//      `bit_rt_select_alloc`. `root.zig` is structurally immune: `chan.zig` is
//      Zig, Zig carries no back-edge polls, and no collection can occur inside
//      a channel operation there at all.
//
//      So the running task's stack needs the same conservative treatment class 4
//      gives every other task's. The low bound is the LIVE `sp`, not the
//      snapshot's `fp`: frame pointers grow toward higher addresses, so
//      `[fp, top)` is the CALLER frames and excludes the innermost frame's own
//      locals — which is precisely where the raw `int` sits. That distinction is
//      MEASURED, not argued (`tests/stress/quicwire` under `BIT_GC=stress`,
//      12 packets over channels with `select`): bad-packet rate 2 of 8 with no
//      class 8, 1 of 12 bounded at `fp`, 0 of 14 bounded at `sp`.
//
//      IT IS NOT FREE, unlike every class above it. Under `BIT_GC=stress` —
//      a collection at every safepoint — quicwire goes from ~50s to ~157s,
//      because this walks the live stack on every collection instead of only
//      the frames a stack map names. That is a test policy, not production, but
//      the cost is real and belongs in the record rather than in a surprise.
//
// Classes 4, 5, 7 and 8 are reachable only when a scheduler block is bound;
// class 1 only when a channel registry is. `stwBind` takes all three addresses TOGETHER
// and `stwCanCollect` refuses to collect unless every one of them is present —
// deliberately, because "collect with a smaller root set" is the failure mode
// this whole comment exists to prevent. An unbound runtime does not collect; it
// never collects PARTIALLY.

import { worldPark, worldRendezvous, worldRestart } from "../gc"
import { worldLockTry, worldLockRelease, worldParkedFrame } from "../gc"
import { worldEnsureSelfOn } from "../gc"
import { worldEnsureSelf, maxMutators } from "../gc"
import { gcShouldCollect, gcMarkBegin, gcCollectFinish } from "../gc"
import { gcStatCollections, gcStatTotalSwept, gcNumObjects, gcStatBadOffsets } from "../gc"
import { gcMarkConservative, gcScanSnapshot } from "../gc"
import { wordAt, loadWord } from "../gc"
// stwPoll's hot path inlines worldStopRequested/gcShouldCollect's bodies
// directly (#1700, avoids a cross-module call); these offsets are what it
// needs to do that. MUST STAY IN LOCKSTEP with the real definitions in
// ../gc/gcworldstop.bit, ../gc/gc.bit and ../alloc/alloc.bit — a drifted copy
// here silently disables collection.
import { worldStopWord } from "../gc"
import { gcHeapAddr, gcCfgEnabled, gcCfgStress, gcNextBytes } from "../gc"
import { heapLiveWord } from "../alloc"
import { chanScanRegistryRoots } from "../chan"
import { taskAt, schedMaxRegistered } from "../sched"
import { schedRegLock, schedRegUnlock, schedRegHead, schedRegNext } from "../sched"
import { ctxSp, ctxWords, taskArg, taskState, taskDone, taskStackBase, taskStackBytes } from "../sched"
// The per-task scratch area's word count (#1679) — see `stwScanScratch`.
import { chanScratchWords } from "../sched"

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// The bound blocks
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// CALLER-OWNED, the convention every ported runtime block follows (`worldBind`,
// `chanBootBind`): a `@nosplit` module cannot own a block another module must
// also see, so the owner (`runtime/root`, which already holds `gcState`) passes
// the addresses in once at boot. Zero until then, and zero means "do not
// collect" rather than "collect with less".

let gcAddr: i64 = 0
let schedAddr: i64 = 0
let chanRegAddr: i64 = 0
// Cached alongside the three above (#1700): stwPoll's hot path reads this
// directly instead of calling `worldBlockAddr`, avoiding a cross-module call.
let worldAddr: i64 = 0

// Collections given up because the stop-the-world rendezvous expired —
// `gc.zig`'s `stats.stw_abandoned`, kept here rather than in the collector block
// because that block is full (`gcWords` = 37, every word assigned) and its size
// is `runtime/root`'s declaration. Abandoning is SAFE (skipping a collection
// only costs heap growth) but is evidence that some thread blocks without the
// `blocked` contract, so it is counted and readable.
let stwAbandoned: i64 = 0

// Statically bounded conservative stack scan (Power of 10): 32MB of words, far
// above the 64KB task stacks the scheduler maps.
const stwMaxStackWords = 4194304

// Bind the three process-wide blocks a collection needs. Called once at boot by
// `runtime/root`, after it has `ptrOf`'d its own collector state, scheduler and
// channel registry (which this `@nosplit` module cannot do for it). The World
// block goes to `worldBind` separately — `../gc/gcworld.bit` owns that one.
export @nosplit @symbol("bit_rt_port_stw_bind") function stwBind(g: int, sched: int, chanReg: int, world: int) {
  gcAddr = g
  schedAddr = sched
  chanRegAddr = chanReg
  worldAddr = world
}

// Abandoned-collection count, so a test can assert it stayed 0.
export @nosplit @symbol("bit_rt_port_stw_abandoned") function stwAbandonedCount(): int {
  let n = stwAbandoned
  return n
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// The `BIT_GC_STATS` readings (#1639)
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// `gc.zig`'s `collect` writes `[bit-gc] collection N: ...` to stderr itself.
// THIS PORT CANNOT: every function that can reach a collection is `@nosplit`
// (`runtime/gc` deviation 6 and this file's pin-cycle note), and `@nosplit`
// admits neither `print`, nor an `extern` call, nor a `syscall` (E0075) — so
// nothing on the collector's own path can emit a byte. The line's OWNER is
// therefore the boot code, which is ordinary Bit and has a write primitive, and
// what it needs from here is the numbers.
//
// They are read off the BOUND collector block rather than passed a `g`, because
// the boot module must not be handed the collector state pointer just to print;
// `stwBind` already gave this module the address. Zero before `stwBind`, which
// is the same "nothing to report" answer as zero collections.
//
// The three are pinned rather than re-exported from `runtime/gc`: a
// `--freestanding` module may only reference PINNED symbols (#1628), and
// `runtime/gc`'s observability accessors carry no pin (they are read in-module
// and by same-target stress tests). Pinning them there would widen `runtime/gc`'s
// ABI surface for one caller; forwarding here costs three one-line bodies.

// Collections completed by this collector — `Stats.collections`.
export @nosplit @symbol("bit_rt_port_stw_collections") function stwCollections(): int {
  if (gcAddr == 0) {
    return 0
  }
  return gcStatCollections(wordAt(gcAddr))
}

// Objects swept over the whole run — `Stats.total_swept`. Zero across a run with
// a non-zero collection count means the collector RAN and reclaimed nothing,
// which is the failure a bare count check cannot tell from success.
export @nosplit @symbol("bit_rt_port_stw_swept") function stwSwept(): int {
  if (gcAddr == 0) {
    return 0
  }
  return gcStatTotalSwept(wordAt(gcAddr))
}

// Objects the collector currently holds — the `live=` field.
//
// OBJECT COUNT, NOT `gcLiveBytes`, and the reason is mechanical rather than a
// preference: `gcLiveBytes` reaches the heap through `runtime/gc`'s own private
// `heapBlock`, and the inliner splices its body into this module, so forwarding
// it makes THIS object reference `m2$heapBlock` — a mangled cross-module symbol
// no sibling can define, which `--freestanding` refuses outright (measured).
// The three accessors above and this one are plain word loads, which inline
// cleanly. A count answers the question the stats line is for — did live data
// actually fall across a collection — as directly as a byte total.
export @nosplit @symbol("bit_rt_port_stw_live_objects") function stwLiveObjects(): int {
  if (gcAddr == 0) {
    return 0
  }
  return gcNumObjects(wordAt(gcAddr))
}

// Pointer-map offsets the tracer REFUSED — `Stats.bad_offsets`, the counter
// `../gc/gcmark.bit`'s `scanObject` bumps when a declared field offset is
// misaligned or runs past the body, and then SKIPS the field rather than
// reading out of bounds (`runtime/gc` deviation 5; `gc.zig` asserts instead,
// which compiles out of a release build).
//
// Forwarded here for the same reason the three counters above are, and it is
// the one whose absence was measured: #1679's verification bar asked for
// `bad=0` from a real run and could not get it, because nothing outside a unit
// test could read the counter (#1682). A degraded trace — fields silently
// skipped, so a live referent is never marked and is swept — reads EXACTLY like
// a healthy one at every other observation point, which is the same silent
// shape as a missing root class.
//
// A plain word load through `gcStatBadOffsets`, so it inlines cleanly and this
// object gains no reference to `runtime/gc`'s private heap block — the trap
// recorded on `stwLiveObjects` above.
export @nosplit @symbol("bit_rt_port_stw_bad_offsets") function stwBadOffsets(): int {
  return stwBadOffsetsOn(gcAddr)
}

// The same read over a caller-supplied collector block (#1836), for a program
// driving a private one. Without it such a program reads the LIVE runtime's
// counter through this accessor and its assertion is vacuous — measured:
// `tests/stress/stwcollect` phase F went `bad=true` -> `bad=false` the moment it
// stopped calling `stwBind`, because `gcAddr` was then the live collector's and
// the malformed TypeInfo it had just traced was in its own.
export @nosplit @symbol("bit_rt_port_stw_bad_offsets_on") function stwBadOffsetsOn(gcA: int): int {
  if (gcA == 0) {
    return 0
  }
  return gcStatBadOffsets(wordAt(gcA))
}

// Whether a collection is even permissible: every root source must be
// reachable. See "THE ROOT SET" above — this is the guard that keeps a
// partially-bound runtime from running a collection with a smaller root set
// than it has roots. Inlined directly into stwPoll's hot path (#1700, was a
// standalone `stwCanCollect`, now dead — the checks are duplicated there).

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Root enumeration
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

// Conservatively mark every aligned word in `[sp, top)` — `root.zig`'s
// `conservativeStack`. Every word goes through `gcMarkConservative`, which
// validates against the heap bounds and an exact ownership test before decoding
// anything, so a small integer that looks like a pointer is simply ignored.
@nosplit function stwScanStackRange(g: *i64, sp: int, top: int) {
  if (sp <= 0) {
    return
  }
  // Align UP, as `std.mem.alignForward` does: a misaligned base would read
  // words straddling two slots, which are not object references in any case.
  let a = (sp + 7) / 8 * 8
  let n = 0
  // Bounded by the mapped stack AND by `stwMaxStackWords`.
  while (a + 8 <= top && n < stwMaxStackWords) {
    gcMarkConservative(g, loadWord(a))
    a = a + 8
    n = n + 1
  }
}

// Class 6: one task's per-task SCRATCH area (`runtime/sched/scratch.bit`), the
// `chanScratchWords` words at the BASE of its stack. THIS CLASS HAS NO
// COUNTERPART IN `root.zig` AND IT IS NOT AN OPTIMISATION — it is the root class
// this port needs and the Zig runtime does not, because the port had to put two
// live references somewhere Zig keeps them in places the existing classes
// already cover (#1679):
//
//   - `scrElem` (word 5) is the value slot of a BLOCKING channel send/receive.
//     `chanops.bit` writes the sent value there, points the waiter at it and
//     parks; `chanwrap.bit` receives INTO it and reads the result back out of
//     it. `chan.zig` keeps that slot as an ordinary LOCAL in the parked task's
//     Zig frame, which lives inside `[sp, stackTop)` and is therefore covered by
//     class 4. The port's slot is not: the scratch is at `stackBase`, and a
//     stack grows DOWN from `stackTop`, so the whole scratch area sits strictly
//     BELOW `ctx.sp` — outside class 4's range by construction, for every task,
//     always.
//   - `scrErr` (word 7) is the pending-error object (ABI.md §13). `root.zig`
//     holds it in a `threadlocal`, which Mach-O refuses (§11.11), so SEAM 4
//     moved it here.
//
// Neither is named by any stack map either, so the PRECISE walk (classes 2/3)
// cannot see them and this must cover the RUNNING task as well as parked ones.
// The symptom when it is missing is the one this file's "ROOT SET" header warns
// about: no crash at the point of failure, a live value swept, and a wrong
// answer some time later.
//
// Conservative and whole-area rather than field-by-field: words 8..264 are
// chan-owned waiter nodes and select index arrays whose contents are slot
// ADDRESSES and case indices, and `gcMarkConservative` rejects anything that is
// not an object base, so scanning them costs a bounded walk and cannot retain
// more than a stack scan already can. Enumerating only words 5 and 7 would also
// make this file depend on a layout `runtime/chan` is free to extend.
// BOUNDED BY THE TASK'S OWN MAPPING, NOT ONLY BY `chanScratchWords`. Every task
// the scheduler creates has a `schedStackBytes` (64 KiB) stack, so the scratch
// always fits — but a caller-supplied block need not, and
// `tests/stress/stwcollect` supplies a 64-WORD synthetic stack. Reading a fixed
// 265 words off that base walks 201 words past the array and SIGSEGVs, which is
// how this bound was found rather than reasoned about. `min` of the two is the
// same walk for every real task and a safe one for a synthetic block.
@nosplit function stwScanScratch(g: *i64, t: *i64) {
  let base = *(t + taskStackBase)
  if (base <= 0) {
    return
  }
  let n = *(t + taskStackBytes) / 8
  if (n > chanScratchWords) {
    n = chanScratchWords
  }
  let i = 0
  // Bounded: at most `chanScratchWords` words, and never past the mapping.
  while (i < n) {
    gcMarkConservative(g, loadWord(base + i * 8))
    i = i + 1
  }
}

// Class 7: a parked task's OWN saved callee-saved register file — `t[0
// .. ctxWords)`, the block `schedSwitch` (`../sched/sched.bit`) writes on
// every park. THIS CLASS HAS NO `root.zig` COUNTERPART EITHER, and unlike
// class 6 it is not an addition — it is a CORRECTION of class 4's own
// soundness argument above ("sound because a parked task's callee-saved
// references are all spilled by the runtime call chain that parked it").
//
// That argument holds for `sched.zig`'s oracle, where LLVM's ordinary
// callee-saved spill for a value still live across a call lands ON THE
// CALLEE'S STACK FRAME — inside `[sp, stackTop)`, so class 4's plain stack
// walk already sees it. It does NOT hold here. `schedSwitch` is a
// hand-written `asm` block, not an ordinary prologue: it saves x19..x28
// (arm64) / rbx,r12..r15 (x64) into the TASK'S OWN CONTROL BLOCK — a
// separate allocation from its stack — precisely because the whole point of
// a context switch is to leave nothing usable behind on the stack it is
// vacating. A value the register allocator keeps live in one of those
// registers across a park (a `string`'s pointer held across a `sleep()` in
// a retry loop is the case that found this) is therefore saved OUTSIDE
// `[ctxSp, stackTop)` and invisible to `stwScanStackRange` — collected out
// from under a task that has not finished with it, then silently reused.
//
// Scanned unconditionally over all 22 words rather than picking out the
// GP-only slots: `gcMarkConservative` already rejects anything that is not
// an exact heap object base (sp/pc/fp/lr and the FP file d8..d15 all fail
// that test harmlessly), so a blanket walk costs a few wasted probes and
// needs no per-architecture slot list to go stale.
@nosplit function stwScanCtx(g: *i64, t: *i64) {
  let i = 0
  while (i < ctxWords) {
    gcMarkConservative(g, *(t + i))
    i = i + 1
  }
}

// Classes 4, 5, 6 and 7: every registered task's `arg`, scratch area and
// saved register file, and every task's stack except the one this thread is
// running (scanned precisely from the snapshot instead) and any finished
// task (its stack holds nothing live). `sched.zig`'s `forEachTaskArg` +
// `forEachOtherStack`, fused into one walk because they visit the same list
// under the same lock, plus the port-only scratch and ctx classes documented
// above.
// The live stack pointer, the same one-instruction form as
// `../sched/sched.bit`'s `readSp` — inline by construction, so legal in a
// `@nosplit` body. Duplicated rather than imported because that one is module
// private and this is a different module; the instruction is the contract.
//
// Reading it in a callee is safe for class 8's purpose: a callee's prologue only
// moves `sp` DOWN within the same stack, so the answer is never HIGHER than the
// real boundary, and a low bound that is too low only widens a conservative
// scan.
@nosplit function stwReadSp(): int {
  return asm {
    arm64 { 0x910003E0 }              // mov x0, sp
    x64   { 0x48, 0x89, 0xE0 }        // mov rax, rsp
    result arm64 x0 x64 rax : int
  }
}

// Is `sp` inside this task's own stack mapping? That, and NOT
// `schedCurrentTask()`, is what makes a task "the one this thread is running"
// for classes 4/7/8 — because the only thing those three classes actually care
// about is WHICH STACK WE ARE STANDING ON.
//
// `schedCurrentTask()` answers a related but different question: it reads the
// task pointer stored at the 64KiB-aligned base of the current stack. Keying off
// it would make class 8 silently inert whenever it returns 0 — a thread whose
// stack carries no such pointer, which is every thread when the process runs on
// a Zig-sourced archive, and the bootstrap thread before the first green task
// exists. The running stack still holds raw `int` references in those cases, so
// "no green task here" must not mean "nothing to scan".
//
// Deriving it from `sp` also makes the class TESTABLE, which keying off the
// stack-base pointer does not: a harness cannot choose what
// `schedCurrentTask()` returns, but it can register a task whose stack mapping
// spans the stack the harness is standing on. `tests/stress/stwcollect`'s
// phase H does exactly that.
@nosplit function stwTaskHoldsSp(t: *i64, sp: int): bool {
  let base = *(t + taskStackBase)
  if (base <= 0) {
    return false
  }
  return sp >= base && sp < base + *(t + taskStackBytes)
}

@nosplit function stwScanTasks(g: *i64, sched: *i64) {
  let sp = stwReadSp()
  schedRegLock(sched)
  let addr = schedRegHead(sched)
  let n = 0
  // Bounded by `schedMaxRegistered`, `sched.zig`'s own runaway backstop.
  while (addr != 0 && n < schedMaxRegistered) {
    let t = taskAt(addr)
    gcMarkConservative(g, *(t + taskArg))
    // Every task, including the running one and a finished one: a `done` task's
    // STACK holds nothing live, but its scratch still holds whatever the last
    // operation left in `scrElem`/`scrErr`, and the running task's scratch is
    // invisible to the precise walk.
    stwScanScratch(g, t)
    if (*(t + taskState) != taskDone) {
      let running = stwTaskHoldsSp(t, sp)
      // Class 8 (#1832): the RUNNING task's low bound is the LIVE sp. `ctxSp`
      // is stale for it — that is where it last parked, not where it is.
      let lo = *(t + ctxSp)
      if (running) {
        lo = sp
      }
      stwScanStackRange(g, lo, *(t + taskStackBase) + *(t + taskStackBytes))
      // The running task's own registers are live CPU state, not a saved
      // block — scanning `t[0..ctxWords)` for it would read whatever this
      // task's context slots last held (stale, from its previous park) and
      // is neither needed (its real registers are covered by the precise
      // snapshot walk) nor safe to treat as current. Gated identically to
      // the stack range for that reason, not by accident of placement.
      if (!running) {
        stwScanCtx(g, t)
      }
    }
    addr = schedRegNext(t)
    n = n + 1
  }
  schedRegUnlock(sched)
}

// One full stop-the-world mark-sweep. The world is already stopped and this
// thread holds the world lock; `snap` is this thread's own `SafepointFrame`.
//
// `gcMarkBegin` .. `gcCollectFinish` is `gc.zig`'s `collect` with the root
// callback inverted (`../gc/gccollect.bit` deviation 2) — everything between the
// two is the root enumeration, in `root.zig`'s `scanRoots` order.
@nosplit function stwCollect(g: *i64, world: *i64, selfSlot: int, snap: int, sched: int, chanReg: int) {
  gcMarkBegin(g)

  // 1. Buffered `is_ref` channel elements (§11).
  chanScanRegistryRoots(g, wordAt(chanReg))

  // 2. This thread's own frames, precisely (§4).
  gcScanSnapshot(g, snap)

  // 3. Every other stopped OS thread's frames, precisely.
  let i = 0
  // Bounded: exactly `maxMutators` slots.
  while (i < maxMutators) {
    if (i != selfSlot) {
      let f = worldParkedFrame(world, i)
      if (f != 0) {
        gcScanSnapshot(g, f)
      }
    }
    i = i + 1
  }

  // 4 + 5. Every other task's stack, conservatively, and every task's `arg`.
  stwScanTasks(g, taskAt(sched))

  gcCollectFinish(g)
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// The poll body — `safepointImpl` (root.zig:638), IN FULL
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// `snap` is the address of the caller's `SafepointFrame` (ABI.md §4), which the
// naked shim below reserved on THIS thread's own stack. Per-thread by
// construction — no TLS, no registration, no ordering — which is the #1429 fix;
// the frame used to be one process-wide global and two workers polling at once
// overwrote each other's registers and then both collected.
//
// The frame is valid for exactly as long as this call: it lives below the
// shim's `sp`, so publishing it (`worldPark`) is sound only while this thread
// stays inside the shim, which parking guarantees.
//
// Exactly one of three things happens (ABI.md §5):
//
//  1. Someone else is collecting -> park here until they finish. A mutator that
//     ignored this would run behind the collector's back.
//  2. Nobody is collecting and the trigger is not crossed -> return. The hot
//     path: one atomic load plus the trigger test.
//  3. The trigger is crossed -> try to become the collector.
//
// The `tryAcquire` LOSER PARKS rather than spinning on the lock. That is the
// deadlock-freedom argument, and it is why `worldLockTry` exists alongside the
// spinning `worldLockAcquire`: a thread the collector is waiting for is never
// itself waiting for the collector.
//
// `@nosplit` is REQUIRED, not stylistic — see the pin-cycle note above.
export @nosplit @symbol("bit_rt_port_stw_poll") function stwPoll(snap: int) {
  stwPollOn(gcAddr, schedAddr, chanRegAddr, worldAddr, snap)
}

// The same poll over CALLER-SUPPLIED bindings (#1836).
//
// The live path above must read module state: the safepoint shim is `@naked` and
// calls this with nothing but a snapshot, so there is nowhere else for the four
// addresses to come from. A program driving a PRIVATE collector has no such
// constraint, and calling `stwBind`/`worldBind` to get one is not "private" at
// all — those set process-wide bindings, so it replaces the live runtime's for
// every thread. `tests/stress/stwcollect` did exactly that and the live worker
// joined the test's registry as a mutator the test never created and could not
// schedule; the rendezvous waited on it and abandoned (#1833).
//
// This is the line Go draws too. Components owning process-global state are
// tested against the live runtime; components taking their state as a parameter
// get real private instances, and those never write the global — `NewPageAlloc`
// is handed `new(mutex)` and its own stats (`src/runtime/export_test.go`). Go
// has no way to disable runtime startup at all, which is why the alternative of
// an env-gated inert mode was rejected.
//
// Every binding is a parameter, including the World, so a caller can hold one
// the live runtime has never heard of.
export @nosplit @symbol("bit_rt_port_stw_poll_on")
function stwPollOn(gcA: int, schedA: int, chanRegA: int, worldA: int, snap: int) {
  // Registers this thread if it has not registered yet, and parks it there if a
  // stop is already pending. Publishes the REAL snapshot: `root.zig`'s
  // `currentMutator` passes 0 from the allocation door (runtime code, no stack
  // map) but its real frame from the safepoint door, and parking with 0 would
  // hide exactly the references this poll's stack map names.
  let mu = worldEnsureSelfOn(worldA, snap)

  // Inlined `worldBlockAddr` + `wordAt` (#1700): `worldAddr` is this module's
  // own cache (`stwBind`'s 4th param), so this is a plain load instead of a
  // cross-module call.
  let waddr = worldA
  if (waddr == 0) {
    return    // pre-boot: `worldBind` has not run, so there is no registry yet
  }
  let worldNil: *i64 = nil
  let world = worldNil + (waddr / 8)

  // MUST MIRROR `worldStopRequested` (../gc/gcworldstop.bit) — inlined to skip
  // a cross-module call on this hot path.
  if (atomicLoad(world + worldStopWord) != 0) {
    // Someone is collecting. Park until they finish. `worldPark` returns at
    // once if the stop cleared in the gap, which is what makes this safe to
    // call speculatively. A thread with no slot has nothing to publish and
    // nothing waits for it.
    if (mu != 0) {
      worldPark(world, mu - 1, snap)
    }
    return
  }

  // MUST MIRROR the former `stwCanCollect` (see its note above) — inlined for
  // the same reason.
  if (gcA == 0) {
    return
  }
  if (schedA == 0) {
    return
  }
  if (chanRegA == 0) {
    return
  }
  let gNil: *i64 = nil
  let g = gNil + (gcA / 8)

  // MUST MIRROR `gcShouldCollect` (../gc/gccollect.bit) — inlined for the same
  // reason. DRIFT HERE SILENTLY DISABLES COLLECTION: keep this predicate
  // exactly in step with the real one (the cold path below still calls the
  // real function, so both must agree).
  if (*(g + gcCfgEnabled) == 0) {
    return
  }
  if (*(g + gcCfgStress) == 0) {
    let hNil: *i64 = nil
    let hb = hNil + (*(g + gcHeapAddr) / 8)
    if (atomicLoad(hb + heapLiveWord) < *(g + gcNextBytes)) {
      return
    }
  }

  // A thread with no slot must not collect: `worldRendezvous` skips only the
  // collector's own slot, so a slotless collector would wait for itself forever.
  if (mu == 0) {
    return
  }
  let selfSlot = mu - 1

  if (!worldLockTry(world)) {
    // Another thread is collecting, or is about to. Park speculatively — a
    // no-op if it turns out no stop is pending.
    worldPark(world, selfSlot, snap)
    return
  }

  // Re-test UNDER THE LOCK: the thread that just released it may have collected
  // everything we were about to collect for.
  if (gcShouldCollect(g)) {
    if (worldRendezvous(world, selfSlot)) {
      stwCollect(g, world, selfSlot, snap, schedA, chanRegA)
    } else {
      // Could not stop the world within the bound. ABANDON — never force.
      // Skipping a collection only costs heap growth; collecting while a mutator
      // runs costs correctness.
      stwAbandoned = stwAbandoned + 1
    }
    // Restart on BOTH paths: `rendezvous` sets the stop flag before it waits, so
    // an expired rendezvous leaves it set and every mutator that already parked
    // would never resume.
    worldRestart(world)
  }
  worldLockRelease(world)
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// The LIVE entry — AND THE GATE THAT USED TO BE HERE (#1639, removed by #1650)
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// This is what the shim below actually calls. It adds NOTHING to `stwPoll`, and
// the fact that it adds nothing is the fix, so the removed code is recorded here
// rather than deleted silently.
//
// It used to read `if (schedCurrentTask() == 0) { return }` — poll only from a
// green task's stack. That was never a policy; it was a workaround for
// `gcworld.bit` keeping ONE process-wide `curMut` where `root.zig` keeps a
// `threadlocal g_mutator`. With one shared cache, letting the boot thread and a
// worker thread both poll meant both would read and write one slot, and the
// consequence was not a lost collection but a corrupted heap: the boot thread
// would collect using the worker's slot as "its own", and `worldRendezvous` and
// the parked-frame walk both SKIP the collector's own slot, so the worker's
// published frame — the only precise record of the running task's references —
// would never be scanned and every reference in it would be swept.
//
// THE GATE'S OWN COST WAS THE SAME CLASS OF BUG, one level up, and that is #1650.
// `schedCurrentTask()` returns 0 on a worker's OWN OS-thread stack, so a worker's
// run loop never polled. But `schedWorkerRun`'s `nowFn`/`pollFn` are function
// VALUES — `(code, env)` cells on the managed heap (§11.10) — and they live in
// that loop's frames, on exactly the stack the gate excluded. None of
// `stwCollect`'s five root classes covers a worker's own off-task stack, so
// those cells were swept while live and the next call through one branched into
// freed memory: `tests/stress/chanracelinux` faulted 20 runs out of 20 on both
// ELF targets. Zig's `safepointImpl` has no gate and its control run keeps
// `survivors=4` — the four cells (two threads x two closures) its ungated poll
// holds alive.
//
// So the claim "that set is EXACTLY the set Zig's threadlocal admits" was wrong,
// and wrong in the unsafe direction: Zig admits EVERY thread that reaches a
// safepoint, and a worker's scheduling loop is compiled Bit code that reaches
// plenty. `../gc/gcthread.bit` now supplies a real per-OS-thread token, so the
// registry is keyed rather than shared and the gate has nothing left to protect.
//
// KEPT AS A SEPARATE FUNCTION even though it is now a pure forward, because it
// is the pinned symbol the naked shim below materializes with `entryOf` and the
// name the ABI names. Collapsing it into `stwPoll` would also re-merge the split
// `tests/stress/stwcollect` (#1633) depends on, which drives `stwPoll` directly
// with a collector block of its own.
export @nosplit @symbol("bit_rt_port_stw_safepoint") function stwSafepoint(snap: int) {
  stwPoll(snap)
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// `bit_rt_safepoint` — the naked register/frame snapshot shim (ABI.md §4)
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// Zero-arg, `callconv(.c)`, emitted by both backends at every loop back edge.
// Naked so it runs with NO prologue: it captures the caller's return address,
// frame pointer and callee-saved register file BEFORE any compiled code could
// overwrite them, then hands that snapshot to `stwPoll` as its first C argument.
//
// The `SafepointFrame` layout it builds is root.zig's, field for field:
//   +0   ret        the caller's return address (0 would mean "not captured")
//   +8   fp         the caller's frame pointer
//   +16  regs[32]   regs[n] at +16+8n, physical register n's live value
// `regs` is deliberately NOT zeroed — this runs at every back edge and clearing
// 32 words per poll is not free. Sound because ABI.md §4 restricts the registers
// a stack map may name at a safepoint to exactly the callee-saved file saved
// here, and because every word the walk reads goes through the validating
// `gcMarkRoot`.
//
// The reservation is 8 bytes larger than the 272-byte struct on x86-64 and 16
// larger on AArch64, purely to keep the stack ABI-aligned at the call. A function
// containing a safepoint is non-leaf by construction (it calls this), so it may
// not rely on the x86-64 red zone and overlapping that zone costs nothing.
//
// THE PAYLOAD IS HAND-ENCODED AND DISASSEMBLY-CHECKED, instruction for
// instruction against root.zig's `asm volatile` (root.zig:698-750) — there is no
// text assembler in the toolchain (§11.6), and a wrong offset here does not fail
// a build, it publishes the wrong register as a root.
//
//   x86-64 (SysV; rbx=3, r13=13, r14=14, r15=15 -> +40,+120,+128,+136):
//     48 81 EC 18 01 00 00        subq  $280, %rsp
//     4C 8B 84 24 18 01 00 00     movq  280(%rsp), %r8    # the caller's ret addr
//     4C 89 04 24                 movq  %r8, 0(%rsp)
//     48 89 6C 24 08              movq  %rbp, 8(%rsp)
//     48 89 5C 24 28              movq  %rbx, 40(%rsp)
//     4C 89 6C 24 78              movq  %r13, 120(%rsp)
//     4C 89 B4 24 80 00 00 00     movq  %r14, 128(%rsp)
//     4C 89 BC 24 88 00 00 00     movq  %r15, 136(%rsp)
//     48 89 E7                    movq  %rsp, %rdi        # arg 0 = the frame
//     FF D1                       call  *%rcx
//     48 81 C4 18 01 00 00        addq  $280, %rsp
//     C3                          ret
//   At entry `rsp % 16 == 8` (the caller's `call` pushed the return address), so
//   subtracting 280 restores `rsp % 16 == 0` for the call while leaving the
//   return address at 280(%rsp). `rbp` is still the caller's frame pointer — no
//   prologue ran. `rdi`/`r8`/`rcx` are all caller-saved, so materializing them
//   cannot destroy a callee-saved value before it is saved.
//
//   AArch64 (x19..x28 -> +168..+240):
//     D10483FF   sub  sp, sp, #288
//     F90003FE   str  x30, [sp, #0]        # x30 = the caller's return address
//     F90007FD   str  x29, [sp, #8]        # x29 = the caller's frame pointer
//     A90AD3F3   stp  x19, x20, [sp, #168]
//     A90BDBF5   stp  x21, x22, [sp, #184]
//     A90CE3F7   stp  x23, x24, [sp, #200]
//     A90DEBF9   stp  x25, x26, [sp, #216]
//     A90EF3FB   stp  x27, x28, [sp, #232]
//     910003E0   mov  x0, sp               # arg 0 = the frame
//     F81F0FFE   str  x30, [sp, #-16]!
//     D63F0020   blr  x1
//     F84107FE   ldr  x30, [sp], #16
//     910483FF   add  sp, sp, #288
//     D65F03C0   ret
//   288 keeps `sp` 16-aligned (the hardware enforces it). `x0`/`x1` are the first
//   argument and caller-saved scratch, so neither disturbs the saved file. `x30`
//   is preserved across the call by its own push, NOT by the frame copy, so the
//   return path does not depend on the callee leaving the frame untouched.
//
// `input` materializes `entryOf(stwSafepoint)` into `rcx`/`x1` ahead of the
// payload — the gated live entry, not `stwPoll` itself; see its comment —
// the only compiler-emitted instruction in this function, an inline address
// materialization against a link-time constant (§11.10) that touches no other
// register and no memory. `entryOf` rather than a direct call because §11.6 has
// no relocation for a branch OUT of an `asm` block.
//
// PINNED `bit_rt_root_safepoint`, NOT `bit_rt_safepoint`: root.zig still defines
// the real name and claiming it now is a DuplicateSymbol at every link (#1624).
// G2 (#1583) drops the `_root` infix, as for every other pin in this tree.
@naked @symbol("bit_rt_safepoint")
function safepointEntry() {
  return asm volatile {
    x64   { 0x48, 0x81, 0xEC, 0x18, 0x01, 0x00, 0x00,
            0x4C, 0x8B, 0x84, 0x24, 0x18, 0x01, 0x00, 0x00,
            0x4C, 0x89, 0x04, 0x24,
            0x48, 0x89, 0x6C, 0x24, 0x08,
            0x48, 0x89, 0x5C, 0x24, 0x28,
            0x4C, 0x89, 0x6C, 0x24, 0x78,
            0x4C, 0x89, 0xB4, 0x24, 0x80, 0x00, 0x00, 0x00,
            0x4C, 0x89, 0xBC, 0x24, 0x88, 0x00, 0x00, 0x00,
            0x48, 0x89, 0xE7,
            0xFF, 0xD1,
            0x48, 0x81, 0xC4, 0x18, 0x01, 0x00, 0x00,
            0xC3 }
    arm64 { 0xD10483FF,
            0xF90003FE,
            0xF90007FD,
            0xA90AD3F3,
            0xA90BDBF5,
            0xA90CE3F7,
            0xA90DEBF9,
            0xA90EF3FB,
            0x910003E0,
            0xF81F0FFE,
            0xD63F0020,
            0xF84107FE,
            0x910483FF,
            0xD65F03C0 }
    input arm64 x1 x64 rcx = entryOf(stwSafepoint)
    clobber x64   { rdi, r8, memory }
    clobber arm64 { x0, memory }
  }
}
